WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:03.830
It's a small body.

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.440
It should have cooled off long ago.

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:07.460
Nothing very interesting is going on.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:11.270
And when we actually got out to Ceres,
nothing could have been further from the

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:11.840
truth.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:20.260
March 2015, NASA's dawn probe arrives at
Ceres.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:27.000
As the Dawn spacecraft pulled up to Ceres, we
saw the craters and the surface that we

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:28.080
expected to see.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.100
And then all of a sudden, something totally
mysterious rotated into view.

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:40.570
One of the craters had two bright spots,
almost like two eyes staring right back at

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:41.050
us.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:48.380
It was such a puzzle to the science
community, because what are these doing here?

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.450
Are they ice? It looks very fresh.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:51.980
What on earth could it be?

00:00:55.990 --> 00:01:00.970
Scientists find over 100 of these mysterious
white spots.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:06.640
The largest is in a 50 mile wide crater
called Occator.

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:13.520
They are unexpectedly made up of a substance
we find on Earth.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.570
Sodium carbonate, a kind of salt.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:23.210
We believe the salts on Ceres are actually
very young.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.460
We think they're as young as 4 million years
old.

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:28.580
And that's basically like yesterday in terms
of geology.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:30.690
And that is super weird, right?

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.650
That's happening not on sort of a geologic
era.

00:01:33.950 --> 00:01:35.810
It's happening now today.

00:01:37.970 --> 00:01:43.040
What could cause patches of salt on a world
long presumed dead?

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:51.240
Planetary geologist Jenny Rathbun believes a
clue might be found at Mono Lake in

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.140
California.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.590
All right, I'm here looking at this beautiful
lake off in the distance and standing on

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.390
massive white deposits.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:02.940
These white deposits used to be a part of
this lake.

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:06.180
At one point, the lake had dissolved a lot
of the materials in it.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.970
And then as it receded, it left behind the
materials as it evaporated away.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:14.700
And these things are salts.

00:02:15.090 --> 00:02:17.610
They're kind of granular in texture.

00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.170
And just to make sure we taste it and yeah,
sure enough, it's salty.

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:29.790
The salt at lake mono crystallizes as the
water evaporates, the only way it can form.

00:02:32.310 --> 00:02:36.990
The researchers believe the same process is
taking place on series.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:42.770
This means there must be liquid water
beneath the surface.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:49.300
But how? Out in the deep freeze of the
asteroid belt.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:53.800
These bright spots are located in the centers
of craters.

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:55.540
They're located around cracks in the
surface.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:01.000
And that is telling us that this material is
coming from under the surface and welling up

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:01.750
onto it.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.990
Absolutely nobody expected there to be liquid
water beneath the surface of Ceres.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.570
We cannot explain what is keeping that water
warm.

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.610
On some moons, gravitational tugging keeps
the interiors warm.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:21.140
But series is not really near anything else
that's very large.

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:25.850
So the amazing thing is that we may not even
understand how rocky planets work.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:30.300
There may be another source of energy,
another mechanism for heating the interior

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.250
that we haven't even discovered yet.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:35.890
To find out how serious has liquid water.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:39.520
We have to rewind the clock 4.6 billion
years.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:47.600
Debris left over from the formation of the
sun slams together to form the dwarf planets.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:56.160
As they take shape, the heavier rocky
material sinks to the center and forms a hot

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.300
molten core.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.740
Slushy water ice floats to the top.

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:09.570
For a while it stays liquid, but once the
core cools, it freezes and forms the solid

00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:10.920
mantle and crust.

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:18.120
That surface should still be solid, so the
salt patches remain a perplexing mystery.

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:23.790
We still haven't answered the question How
could there actually still be liquid water on

00:04:23.790 --> 00:04:25.500
Ceres? That's still a hard question to
answer.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:30.630
One way this could happen is if it's not
actually pure water, if you've mixed it with

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:31.560
something else.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:40.630
Some scientists have proposed that a salty
ocean lies beneath the surface.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.370
The high concentration of salt lowers the
freezing point of the water, keeping it

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:58.110
liquid. When asteroid

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:03.630
impacts fracture the crust, this salty water
oozes up from below.

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:12.680
The liquid swiftly evaporates, but the salt
remains, leaving a brilliant white spot on

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:13.520
the surface.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:19.330
In fact, I'm willing to bet there could be
water coming up now, bringing salts up to the

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:21.670
surface, evaporating way into space.

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:25.660
And that means liquid water is very close to
the surface of Ceres right now.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:30.450
Series has an even more startling card up its
sleeve.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.350
Recent research suggests that it's an
immigrant.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.800
It didn't form anywhere near the asteroid
belt.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:45.240
Ceres may have been born alongside hundreds
of other dwarf planets, many billions of

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:46.950
miles away from the sun.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:50.190
So how did it get here?

00:05:54.860 --> 00:06:01.040
Most of the dwarf planets we've discovered
live far out in the solar system beyond the

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:02.060
orbit of Neptune.

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:08.930
But series orbits between Mars and Jupiter
in the asteroid belt.

00:06:09.820 --> 00:06:12.310
But its location isn't the only hint.

00:06:12.340 --> 00:06:14.380
Ceres might be an interloper.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:25.200
Normally celestial objects are made of the
same materials as the other bodies in their

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.500
neighborhoods. But that's not the case with
Ceres.

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:39.160
The asteroid belt is mostly made up of dry,
rocky bodies composed of the same heavy

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:42.460
elements that form the rocky inner planets.

00:06:42.550 --> 00:06:44.950
Series is very different.

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:49.090
Series is essentially an icy world, right?

00:06:49.090 --> 00:06:52.300
It's it's made out of ices instead of rocks.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:56.440
And so that's kind of weird, considering
where it is.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:02.380
The ice on Ceres also contains chemical
compounds that in the early years of the

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:04.900
solar system didn't exist in the asteroid
belt.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:09.070
The more we learned about series, the more
mysterious it became.

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:13.510
One of the things is that series has quite a
lot of ammonia on it, and we don't find

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:16.510
ammonia anywhere near the inner part of the
solar system.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.970
But we do find it in the outer solar system.

00:07:20.150 --> 00:07:22.640
We've detected ammonia on Pluto.

00:07:22.670 --> 00:07:28.100
It's Moon Charon and out in the frozen
Kuiper Belt where we find the other dwarf

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:28.820
planets.

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:34.520
We think that the origin of that ammonia
would have had to be in a very cold part of

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.120
the solar system colder than where we find
Ceres today.

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:45.290
But how an icy dwarf planet with ammonia came
to inhabit a place where ammonia can't form.

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:46.580
That's a huge puzzle.

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:51.710
This suggests to us that Ceres perhaps formed
in the outer solar system and then migrated

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:54.470
inwards to its present location in the
asteroid belt.

00:07:57.630 --> 00:08:04.650
We used to think that planetary orbits were
completely immutable, that they were simply

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:06.780
ran like clockwork and they didn't move
around.

00:08:07.590 --> 00:08:12.930
Now we know that that's not the case in the
early stages of planet formation.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:20.340
Planets move around through the gaseous disk
that encircles the young sun, much like rafts

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:23.250
that are pushed around by ocean currents.

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:30.600
Is ammonia suggests that dwarf planets rafted
around on the cosmic ocean, along with the

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:31.710
Young Planets.

00:08:32.010 --> 00:08:37.320
Series is sort of a smoking gun that solar
systems are much more dynamic, much more

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:38.580
dramatic than we know.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:43.080
There's mounting evidence that Ceres formed
farther out in the solar system and something

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.060
brought this little world in.

00:08:45.270 --> 00:08:46.740
What could possibly have done that?

